3. Capture the exact failing PDF

This is very important for the next production occurrence.

Instead of only:

catch (Exception e) {
    logger.error("PDF merge failed", e);
}

use:

catch (Exception e) {
    logger.error(
        "PDF merge failed for file: {}. Exception: {}",
        pdfFile,
        e.getMessage(),
        e
    );
}

This will tell us whether the annotation error is actually coming from:

72170-4_1002036770_20250617.pdf

or another PDF in the ZIP.

⸻

4. Add validation before declaring merge successful

After:

mergedDoc.save(outputFile);

validate that the output actually exists and can be opened.

For example:

mergedDoc.save(outputFile);
if (!Files.exists(outputFile.toPath())
        || outputFile.length() == 0) {
    throw new IOException(
        "Merged PDF was not created successfully: " + outputFile
    );
}
logger.info(
    "Merged PDF successfully created: {}",
    outputFile
);

Ideally, reopen the generated PDF as an additional validation:

Document validationDoc = new Document(outputFile);
validationDoc.close();

⸻

5. Don’t silently ignore all PDFs

I would not simply do this:

catch (Exception e) {
    // ignore
}

because DHD is delivering healthcare documents.

Instead maintain a list:

List<String> failedFiles = new ArrayList<>();

and:

catch (Exception e) {
    failedFiles.add(pdfFile);
    logger.error(
        "Failed to merge PDF: {}",
        pdfFile,
        e
    );
}

Then after processing:

if (!failedFiles.isEmpty()) {
    logger.warn(
        "PDF merge completed with {} failed file(s): {}",
        failedFiles.size(),
        failedFiles
    );
}

Whether the final ZIP should be delivered when a document is skipped needs to follow the existing Partial Processing/business rule. Don’t introduce document loss just to avoid the exception.

